x86_64-linux  two differences, both recorded elsewhere in this directory. First, on Itanium the name of a member function template encodes the *pattern* signature - a template parameter written T_, a type built from one as an expression - where cxx1 writes the *substituted* signature: clang spells V<M>::head's return S_IXT_EE, cxx1 the substituted S_ILi3EE, and in general any member-template signature that mentions a parameter diverges this way. Microsoft wants the substituted signature and is exact on every such name; a member-template signature with no parameter in it (scaled, plus, nth here, whose returns and arguments are plain int) is exact on every target. Carrying the pattern signature through the member-template mangler, and spelling a non-type parameter as XT_E, is its own step - the same pattern-vs-substituted split free function templates already make, not yet made here for members. Second, the C1/C2 inline constructors clang emits only the C2 of on this target - the difference base-implicit-constructor-emitted.nonames records. What this case is about, member templates run correctly, is checked by run.sh on all three machines.
arm64-darwin  on Itanium the name of a member function template encodes the *pattern* signature - a template parameter written T_ - where cxx1 writes the *substituted* one: clang spells V<M>::head's return S_IXT_EE, cxx1 the substituted S_ILi3EE, and any member-template signature mentioning a parameter diverges this way. Microsoft wants the substituted signature and is exact; a member-template signature with no parameter in it (scaled, plus, nth here) is exact on every target. Carrying the pattern signature through the member-template mangler is its own step, the same split free function templates already make. What this case is about, member templates run correctly, is checked by run.sh on all three machines.
